==English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|kʷyeh₁}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|quiētus||at rest}}. {{doublet|en|quiet}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|kwī.ēʹtəs}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|es}}

# A [[stillness]] or [[pause]]; something that [[quiet]]s or [[repress]]es; removal from [[activity]].
#* {{circa|1600}} [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[s:The Tragedy of Hamlet, Prince of Denmark|The Tragedy of Hamlet]]'', [[s:The Tragedy of Hamlet, Prince of Denmark/Act 3#Scene 1.__A room in the castle.|Act III, Scene 1]]:
#*: {{...}} when he might himself his '''quietus''' make with a bare bodkin?
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} [[death|Death]].
#* '''1886''', [[w:Henry James|Henry James]], ''The Bostonians''.
#*: Olive's specific terrors and dangers had by this time very much blown over; Basil Ransom had given no sign of life for ages, and Henry Burrage had certainly got his '''quietus''' before they went to Europe.
# Final [[settlement]] (e.g., of a debt).

